From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: the guest tone-maps HDR into the display's headroom

What: with the app's HDR switch (OMACVM_GL_EDR=1; OMACVM_GL_HDR=1 from an
app before it), the main output's entry in the layout on OmacVM's display
port carries "edr": the EDR headroom of the window's display now (1 = SDR
white), looked at once a second on the main thread and sent when it moved
by 10 % or more (ui/omacvm-edr.h); 0 when PQ cannot be shown this run
(half-float surfaces failed, or the CAOpenGLLayer path). The guest's
display sync runs Virtual-1 in PQ with max_luminance = 203 nits x that
headroom, or at 10 bits in sRGB without one. The layer's toneMapMode is
never while it shows PQ, so macOS does not tone-map again. Only a
GL_RGB10_A2 scanout (the guest's 10-bit driver) is deep and PQ; a float
scanout goes to 8-bit sRGB surfaces as any other.

Why: macOS's own tone mapping (the default, automatic) maps PQ into the
display's current headroom whenever that is below what the content could
reach, and maps SDR white down with it: measured on the MacBook Pro's XDR
panel at headroom 1, PQ 203 nits (BT.2408 SDR white) read 0.50 of the
Mac's white with automatic or ifSupported, with or without
kIOSurfaceContentHeadroom; with never it read 1.00. The guest's desktop
looked dim whenever the panel was bright. The headroom follows the
panel's brightness, so the guest is told when it moves. Before, QEMU
trusted OMACVM_GL_HDR that every 10-bit frame was PQ and the guest never
heard when PQ could not be shown.

Threads: NSScreen is read on the main thread only (a dispatch timer on the
main queue, started at the guest's hello, only with HDR wanted); the commit
queue only sets the layer's properties (cocoa_gl_layer_follow).
present_deep_failed is set on QEMU's thread and read on the main thread
(qatomic).

OMACVM_GL_EDR_HEADROOM=potential sends the display's most instead of its
current headroom (if macOS kept the current headroom at 1 until content
asks for more).

Upstream: OmacVM only (OmacVM's display port, IOSurface present).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-hdr-headroom.sh (rules, wiring);
src/app/guest/tests/test_display_sync.py (the guest's side); on screen:
work notes tracks/hdr.md (PQ bars, the 10-bit ramp, MacBook Pro XDR).
A new surface depth (the output turning 10-bit) is made at once (at most
one a second), not after
the 0.5 s hold-off for window sizes, so the first PQ frames do not show
in 8-bit sRGB surfaces.

Applies after qemu-cocoa-gl-present-color.patch, omacvm-cocoa-displays.patch
and omacvm-cocoa-hdr-headroom-logic.patch.

